WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.000
Internal network abuse occurs when an application feature that fetches URLs or integrates

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.940
with remote systems can reach services that the attacker cannot access directly.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.720
Payment environments often contain internal services that should not be reachable from

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:20.140
user-controlled input. Cloud metadata exploitation is a serious SSRF scenario.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.160
If a workload can reach metadata endpoints,

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:27.940
attackers may attempt to obtain temporary credentials or environmental information.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.200
Trust boundary violations happen when applications treat user-provided destinations as

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:39.500
safe. URL fetchers, webhook validators, image importers, document converters,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:43.240
and integration testing features all need careful destination controls.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:48.580
SSRF defenses work best when application validation is paired with network segmentation,

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.960
egress filtering, and monitoring for unusual server-initiated traffic.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:57.420
Segment networks and restrict access to internal payment systems.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:02.520
Validate and restrict outbound URLs using allow lists where possible.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:07.380
Apply egress filtering and monitor unusual server-initiated requests.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.120
Selected PCI DSS mappings for this topic include Req.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:18.700
1.3 - Network segmentation and restrictions; Req. 7.2 - Access restrictions; Req.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.120
11.4 - Intrusion detection/prevention.
